  "guards": [
    "Canonical worker-context.json, source blocks, manifest, native context chunks, capsule CIDs, admissions, proof references, signatures, assumptions and constraints are never changed by the presentation layer.",
    "All required_raw_paths and every raw_source_required admission remain literal current-source requirements. Compact views never satisfy or erase those obligations.",
    "Unknown symbols, ambiguous mappings, missing retained blocks, stale selected files or dependencies, foreign scopes and hash mismatches fail before provider dispatch.",
    "Initial source views create no proof, execution, admission or task-completion authority. AST equality is only a rendering check, not proof of behavior or preserved comment semantics.",
    "When editing a previously optional source, require exact raw-source expansion against the current hash before the guarded edit; retain expansion and edit receipts. Ordinary provider terminal tools are not intercepted, so bypassed helper edits cannot qualify as a guarded edit cycle merely because the final task passes.",
    "After a canonical edit, invalidate affected source/view and dependent proof-cache bindings; refresh through the existing semantic context lineage before reusing them.",
    "Historical replay reconstructs original source-read inputs even after canonical edits; it never supplies current operational edit permission."
  ],
  "open_limitations": [
    "Appending read views can improve source availability while increasing initial tokens; net savings remain empirical.",
    "Comment removal can hide useful explanatory information despite equal ASTs; preserve exact raw expansion and report its cost.",
    "Current terminal provider tools allow arbitrary edits; mandatory guarded editing requires a separate native tool boundary or acceptance protocol, not stronger prompt wording.",
    "Whole-repository reversible minification and multi-language edit translation remain out of scope."
  ],
  "proposed_limits": {
